{
  "dom_id": "question-168008",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,\\frac{f(x)+{8}^{2x/5}}{{f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .",
  "worked_solution": "Using the quotient rule, we have \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,\\frac{f(x)+{8}^{2x/5}}{{f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5)}=\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,(f(x)+{8}^{2x/5})}{{lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,({f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5))}.\n$$\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,f(x)=f(\\frac{5}{2})=0$ and ${lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,{f}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(\\frac{5}{2})=\\frac{4}{5}.$ Calculating the limits of the numerator and denominator separately, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,(f(x)+{8}^{2x/5})=0+{8}^{1}=8, \\\\  & {lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,({f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5))=\\frac{4}{5}-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡0=\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the quotient of these limits, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,\\frac{f(x)+{8}^{2x/5}}{{f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5)} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,(f(x)+{8}^{2x/5})}{{lim}_{x\\to 5/2}\\,({f}^{′}(x)-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x-5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{(\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
